### Analytical Commentary on Evaluation Definitions

The canonical evaluation definitions establish the epistemic foundation of the GeminiSupervisor governance framework. Under this model, the system rejects any presumption that an agent's self-generated statements possess evidential value regarding physical execution state. User-authored words (`user_words`) form the primary ground of intent. The effective instruction set (`effective_instruction_U`) aggregates these verbatim instructions with all active system rules and constraints without allowing intermediary summarization to dilute, mutate, or drop user requirements.

The separation between proposal and reality is formalized in `agent_response_P`, which mandates that cognitive intention, practical attempt, execution actions, and verified completion remain strictly delineated. Claims of execution must be validated against `live_ground_truth`—defined exclusively as the freshest authoritative artifact possessing direct ownership of the asserted fact. When live ground truth is unavailable or ambiguous, the evaluation framework enforces explicit classification into `UNKNOWN`, `PARTIAL`, or `NOT_EXECUTED` statuses, expressly prohibiting the promotion of unverified claims into successful gating decisions.

